import"./chunk-GAL4ENT6.js";var r="# Signals + resource()\n\nA contest entry built from Angular's primitives: `signal`, `computed`,\n`linkedSignal`, `resource()` and `rxResource()`. No RxJS state classes of\nanother approach and no NgRx. Judged by the page tests\n(`PAGE_TARGET=resources npm test`) and the e2e suite\n(`E2E_CONTESTER=resources npm run e2e`). Written by an agent, like every\nentry, and also steered by hand by the maintainer.\n\nStatus: see [test_report.md](test_report.md), regenerated by `npm run test:report`.\nFailing scenarios and the tool's gaps are explained in [gaps.md](gaps.md).\n\n## Extra code\n\nCode that is not about the page's domain: caches, batching, status mapping,\nlifetime, written per entity or as generic helpers. Lines from\n[card 06](https://github.com/earthdmitriy/rx-evo/blob/master/docs/contest/results/06-boilerplate.md) (Prettier-formatted, non-blank, non-comment), compared with\nthe four entries (vanilla-rxjs, ngrx-signal-store, resources,\nstateful-observable).\n\n| Measure | Lines | vs average | vs best | vs worst |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Extra code (generic + per-entity) | **351** | \u22129% vs average (388) | +162 vs best | \u2212244 vs worst |\n| Whole data layer | **773** | \u22128% vs average (840) | +472 vs best | \u2212895 vs worst |\n| One more batched lookup like `tiers` | **30** | +56% vs average (19) | +17 vs best | worst |\n\nThe lookup cost has no batching, which this entry does not meet (R-L9): with it, the number would be higher.\n\n## Idea\n\nComponents own their data. A section or field component reads a resource\nfrom its own `rxResource()` or from a shared one. The shared ones come from\nregistries in `data/` (`*.registry.ts`): one per entity type (customers,\norders, order stats, ticket counts, products, prices, stock, carts, cart prices,\nactivity, the customer list, tiers). A registry creates one `rxResource` per key\non the first request and returns the same one afterwards; nothing loads until a\ncomponent asks. The registries are listed in `ResourcesSignedInComponent`'s\n`providers`, so each lives in the signed-in frame's injector and is disposed\nwith it. Resources are bound to their registry and go with it.\n\nA component reads a registry through a `computed`, with the registry call under\n`untracked()` so that creating a resource does not happen inside the reactive\ncontext. A view reads a resource's value only after `hasValue()`, because\n`value()` throws in the error state. The block is `app-resource-block`, which\ntakes the resource itself.\n\nWrites update the shared data and then bump a version in `Bus` (`data/bus.ts`,\nroot-provided counters for orders, order stats, stock and tickets). The\nregistries for those topics key their resources by `(id, version)`, so a bump\nmakes a new key and every reader shares the one new request.\n\nUI state is plain signals in `data/ui-state.ts`. The editor's search text is\ntyped at once and debounced into a query with `toObservable` and `debounce`.\n\nWhere it fits: `resource()` is one async read owned by one component or\nservice. On a simple page, or an isolated part of one, where sections share no\ndata, loading, error, reload, cancel and disposal come with the owner, with no\nextra code. It is not a cache. This page needs shared, cached and invalidated\ndata from stage 2 on, which is why the registries and the `Bus` exist, and\nwhere most failures come from.\n\n## Good\n\n- A `resource()` with `params` cancels its superseded read and skips the loader\n  for `undefined` params, so a section and its field need no code for either\n  (R-C1, R-P5). That holds for component-owned resources; registry entries are\n  the exception (see Bad).\n- Sign-out needs no cache clearing: the frame and its registries are destroyed,\n  and the components that read them with them (R-S3, R-S4).\n- One registry per key gives one request per key for every reader in the\n  session, including the activity list, the tier and the ticket counts\n  (R-L3, R-L10, R-J7).\n\n## Bad\n\nOnly failing requirements and the excess code they cost (spec \xA711). Each is\nexplained in [gaps.md](gaps.md), which also says where `resource()` fits: one\nread per owner, not a shared cache.\n\nGaps of the tool (G1, G2 in `gaps.md`):\n\n- **No shared cache, no invalidation** (R-C4, R-L5, R-L6). The registries keep\n  resources as singletons outside their readers, so a kept entry is never\n  cancelled: `B1` (a superseded customer read keeps running). An order\n  registry broke B8 and was reverted: `C6` (a reopened order loads again).\n  Root-scoped catalog registries broke the page harness and were reverted:\n  `A7` (products and prices requested again after re-login).\n- **No batching** (R-L9, R-J3, R-P3, R-P6): `A2`, `C11` (8 `tiers` and 8\n  `ticketCounts` calls for one list), `C7`, `B10` (one `prices` call per hit),\n  `C12` (26 `prices` calls for a 24-line cart, 3 expected), `D7` (one release\n  shows only the first hit's price), `D9` (line prices and the cart total are\n  separate calls; likely the same cause).\n\nImplementation misses (not tried):\n\n- **Version keys instead of reload** (R-C3, R-W10): `B3` (the Orders tab is\n  `loading`, not `reloading`, after a save) and `C16` (stock asked for 3\n  untouched skus). One `Bus` counter per entity makes a new, empty resource\n  for every key. Calling `reload()` on the registry entries a write changed\n  would keep the value and touch only those keys.\n- **No value kept on a failed poll** (R-G4): `E7`. A failed `reload()` puts the\n  resource in `error`, where `value()` throws, so the activity list goes.\n  Keeping the last value next to the resource would fix it.\n- **Notifications polled once in a minute** (R-L8): `C9` (1 request in 60 s, 2\n  expected). Not traced.\n\nExcess code, even where everything passes:\n\n- **Registry plumbing:** twelve registries (289 lines, the same shape each) and\n  the `untracked()` wrapper at each consumer, for R-L3, R-L10 and R-J7.\n- **Version keys:** the `Bus` counters for R-W3 and R-W10; every reader of a\n  write-affected key folds in the same version.\n- **`hasValue()` guards:** every view reads a resource with `hasValue()` before\n  `value()`, for R-E1. Without them a failed resource throws in its template.\n\n## Requirements\n\n| Requirement | How it is met |\n| --- | --- |\n| R-S1 | helper (`data/ui-state.ts`): `signedIn` signal; the console shows the sign-in form until it is set |\n| R-S2 | helper: the agent and notifications components create their own resources when they are created |\n| R-S3 | helper (`resources-signed-in.component.ts`): the frame's injector is destroyed on sign-out, with its registries and resources |\n| R-S4 | helper: the same frame; registries are provided by it, so no cache survives sign-out (except root `Bus`, which holds counters only) |\n| R-S5 | helper (`data/ui-state.ts`): the tab is kept in root `UiState`; the customer and the panel reset |\n| R-D1 | helper: a section reads its key's registry resource (or its own `rxResource` for params) |\n| R-D2 | helper: the notifications poll is a timer in its component |\n| R-B1 | built in: `app-resource-block` takes the resource and shows its loading and error state |\n| R-J1 | helper: names, prices and stock come from their own registries; a view with no value shows nothing |\n| R-J2 | helper: each field reads the registry resource for its own id |\n| R-J3 | helper: batch calls use the backend's `MAX_BATCH` for the cart total; other lookups are one id per call |\n| R-J4 | helper (`cart-prices.registry.ts`): cart and hit prices are the current price of their sku |\n| R-J5 | helper (`order-panel-section.component.ts`): the linked ticket is read from the `ticketsByOrder` resource |\n| R-J6 | not in the data layer: the shared views write sentences from kind, refs and the name |\n| R-J7 | helper: one registry per counter (`ticket-counts`, `order-stats`, `tiers`) |\n| R-C1 | built in: `rxResource` cancels a superseded read when its `params` change (component-owned resources only) |\n| R-C2 | built in: a changed `params` puts the resource in loading, and the block shows loading while it holds no value |\n| R-W1 | helper (`data/action.ts`): `createAction` publishes pending and error state to the control |\n| R-W2 | helper: the control is disabled while the action is pending |\n| R-W3 | helper (`data/bus.ts`): a bump of the topic makes a new `(id, version)` key, so readers refetch once |\n| R-W5 | helper: the frame's injector is destroyed on sign-out, so a write answered in a later session is not shown |\n| R-W6 | not in the data layer: the shared order view offers Create return |\n| R-W7 | not used: no optimistic display |\n| R-B2 | built in: `app-resource-block` replaces the spinner block in every section |\n| R-E1 | helper: `hasValue()` guards every view, so a failed resource shows its error block |\n| R-E2 | built in: a new `params` value starts a new load; a registry key in error recovers on `reload()` |\n| R-E3 | helper: a failed write keeps the user's input (`action.ts`) |\n| R-G1 | helper: the block's error comes from the main resource's `error()` |\n| R-G2 | helper: the block shows the raw error; the error names no source (the test checks only the error view) |\n| R-G3 | helper: each section and field has its own resource and block, so a failure stays where it happens |\n| R-G4 | **not met**: `E7` \u2014 a failed poll blanks the activity list; `resource()` clears its value on error |\n| R-G5 | helper: `reload()` of the failed resource only |\n| R-G6 | helper: a field's own error block with Reload (`app-resource-block`) |\n| R-C3 | **not met**: `B3` \u2014 a same-key reload changes the registry key, so the value clears |\n| R-C4 | **not met** for registry entries (`B1`); built in for component-owned resources (see R-C1) |\n| R-W8 | helper: writes are not tied to a component's lifetime, so a navigation does not stop them |\n| R-W9 | helper: the write's error keeps the input |\n| R-W10 | helper (`data/bus.ts`, the registries): the write bumps the topics it changed; **not met**: `C16` \u2014 one stock counter for all skus refreshes every shown sku's stock |\n| R-L1 | helper: no resource exists without the signed-in frame, so nothing is requested signed out |\n| R-L2 | built in: a resource loads on its first read, and a registry loads nothing until a component asks |\n| R-L3 | helper: one registry entry per key, shared by every reader |\n| R-L4 | helper (`order-panel-section.component.ts`): `toObservable` plus `debounce` for the editor search |\n| R-L11 | helper: sentence names come from the customer and agent registries |\n| R-L9 | **not met**: `A2`, `C7`, `C11`, `C12`, `B10` \u2014 one request per id; the batch count fails |\n| R-L10 | helper: one registry entry per key, shared |\n| R-L5 | helper: keys in the registries stay cached for the session; **not met** for orders (`C6`) |\n| R-L6 | **not met**: `A7` \u2014 the catalog registries are disposed on sign-out |\n| R-L7 | helper: a write bumps the version of the topic it changed, so its keys refetch |\n| R-L8 | **not met**: `C9` \u2014 1 notifications request in 60 s where 2 are expected; not traced |\n| R-P1 | built in: independent resources load in parallel, each section shows when its own resource settles |\n| R-P2 | helper: the in-flight orders list and each row's shipment are separate resources |\n| R-P3 | **not met** for hits: `D7` \u2014 each hit's price is its own call, so one release shows only the first hit's price |\n| R-P4 | built in: a dependent resource's `params` start it when its input exists |\n| R-P5 | built in: `params` that return `undefined` skip the loader (`status` is `idle`) |\n| R-P6 | **not met**: `D9` \u2014 line prices and the cart total are separate calls, so one release does not show both (likely the batching gap) |\n\n## Tradeoffs\n\nEight criteria, the same in every entry's README. Each is compared across the entries in [docs/contest/results](https://github.com/earthdmitriy/rx-evo/blob/master/docs/contest/results/criteria.md); the numbers below come from there (line counts: card [06](https://github.com/earthdmitriy/rx-evo/blob/master/docs/contest/results/06-boilerplate.md), Prettier-formatted, non-blank, non-comment).\n\n- **Eager or lazy start.** Lazy. An `rxResource` loads on its first read, and a registry creates a resource on its first `customer(id)` call (`data/*.registry.ts`). Gives: no request before a component reads. Costs: creation happens inside a read path, so every consumer wraps the registry call in `untracked()` (`customer-tier.component.ts`).\n- **One request per key, and how long a value lives.** One resource per key in a registry's `Map`, alive for the signed-in frame (`resources-signed-in.component.ts`). No refcount, no eviction. Gives: one request per key. Costs: a superseded shared read keeps running (B1), and each write version leaves its old resource in the `Map` until logout.\n- **Cancelling a superseded read.** Built in for component-owned resources: a changed `params` cancels the previous load. Not for registry entries (B1). Costs: two behaviours to know about, one per kind of resource.\n- **Batching.** None, except the cart total, which is one resource over its skus (`cart-total.component.ts`). Costs: every batch endpoint needs its own shaping in the component.\n- **Keeping data on error or reload.** A resource clears its value when it fails or its key changes, so stale data is not kept (E7, B3). Gives: no stale value can be shown by mistake. Costs: a failed poll blanks its list.\n- **Boilerplate to add one value.** One more batched lookup like `tiers` costs 30 lines in 3 files (without batching, which is not met), most of it a new registry. The data layer is 773 lines: 31 generic, 320 per entity (the 12 registries), 422 domain. Costs: the registry file is the largest single piece of a shared value.\n- **Where state lives.** Component-owned resources and the frame's providers. Root holds only the `Bus` version counters and `UiState`. Gives: a value's lifetime follows where it is provided. Costs: that lifetime is set in one provider list, not next to the read.\n- **Dependency on a library.** Angular's `resource()` and `rxResource()` only. Gives: no third-party API. Costs: the behaviour is whatever Angular's resource implementation does, including `value()` throwing in the error state.\n\n## Try it\n\n- Select a customer, switch to another, and switch back: the first customer's\n  profile, orders and activity come back from the session's registries without\n  a request, and the first customer's reads keep running (B1).\n- Open an order, edit items, search the catalog and add a hit: the hit and the\n  new line briefly share a sku, then the search clears itself.\n- Save an order: the Orders tab, the rail and the KPIs refresh because the\n  version they read changed, not because their data was written directly.\n- Turn on **Throw** for a service in the request panel: the section it feeds\n  fails. Only a later poll or Retry brings its content back; nothing is kept\n  meanwhile.\n";export{r as default};
